This Arduino program controls a 6-servo robotic arm using two analog joysticks and one push-button (SW).
Hardware used: Arduino UNO, 2 standard analog joysticks, 6× small 9-g servo motors.

Joystick Control:

    Joystick 1 controls Servo 1 (Y-axis) and Servo 2 (X-axis).

    Joystick 2 normally controls Servo 3 (Y-axis) and Servo 4 (X-axis).

Button Mode Switch:

    If SW is NOT pressed (sw == 1) → Joystick 2 moves Servo 3 & Servo 4 (default).

    If SW IS pressed (sw == 0) → Joystick 2 instead moves Servo 5 & Servo 6.

    So the second joystick switches between two servo pairs depending on the button.

Movement Logic:

    Joystick values are read from 0–1023.

    A deadzone avoids jitter.

    Small or large joystick movement gives different increments.

    All servo angles are kept between 0° and 180°.

    A smoothMove() function slowly transitions servos to their target angles to reduce jerking.

Summary:

-Two joysticks → control six servos.

-Button → switches the second joystick between two servo groups.

-Smooth movement → reduced servo jitter.

-Serial output prints debug values once per second.